WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.420
Andthropics latest threat report details how sophisticated actors attempted cyber attacks, influence ops, and bioweapons with Claude, and how every attempt was stopped. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 170. The company issued its most detailed public account of real misuse attempts involving the model. Those attempts spanned five areas including cyber attacks, influence operations, surveillance, biology work, and weapons development.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:37.360
Every single operation was detected and halted before it advanced. Insights from the incidents led to updated safeguards inside the system.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:52.020
The same findings went to government agencies and peer labs to help spot matching patterns elsewhere. The cases highlighted are the most advanced examples seen so far, not everyday interactions, and they point to future threat directions.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:01:00.740
Making the full document public lets other platforms watch for the same signals and gives outsiders a clearer picture of how misuse is evolving.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:18.720
A fresh benchmark called CMNI, supplies 13,000 annotated instances drawn from Chinese military news. It applies joint labels across seven event types, ten argument roles, seven entity types, and eight relation types in a single schema.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:26.440
The task requires models to pull triggers, arguments, entities, and relations together rather than handling them separately.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:36.580
Tests on a held-out set covered supervised information extraction models, zero-shot large language models, and fine-tuned large language model extractors.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.180
The benchmark proves especially hard for relation extraction and for matching event argument spans exactly.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:57.620
Zero-shot models often locate relevant meaning yet miss the precise gold boundaries. Researchers created 93 Urdu stories using GPT 5.1, Quen3 Max, and DeepSeq 3.1.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.840
They then labeled errors across nine categories that cover linguistic, semantic, and cultural issues.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:11.620
The outputs showed basic grammar and meaning mistakes, broken story flow, repeated phrasing, and thin cultural detail.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:17.540
Few-shot prompting eased some problems but left most cultural and contextual errors in place.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:25.380
The study treats Urdu as a stand-in for other low resource languages to reveal current limits in multilingual generation.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:37.260
A prompt-based approach for minimal edit grammatical error correction combined taxonomy instructions, batching of uncorrected sentences, and LLM assisted prompt tuning.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:44.500
It reached an F0.5 score of 78.32 on the BEA 2019 set.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:52.960
That result sits just 0.38 points below the fine-tuned single model state of the art without any fine-tuning step.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.080
Batching lowers the overall edit rate across model families by spreading attention.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.460
The taxonomy instructions enforce minimal edit rules through a full list of grammatical error categories.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:09.640
The complete prompts, code, and outputs are now available for anyone to use.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:17.160
Search Atlas turns long agent search paths into graphs that track how evidence travels from each query to the final answer.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:27.420
An automated pipeline scores 86.0% mean edge F1 against human annotated graphs and stays stable across repeated runs.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:34.560
Five agents were evaluated on three benchmarks showing clear differences in search scale and evidence aggregation.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:42.160
Process failures tracked more closely with wrong answers than LLM judges did when looking only at raw traces.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:50.660
The graphs reveal fragmented support, unreachable question constraints, and unverified parametric knowledge slipping into responses.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:04:03.740
Autorexis runs parallel experiments across servers using distributed asynchronous execution, cross-server persistent memory, and separation of natural language skill files from deterministic scripts.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:10.640
A dual loop design lets execution outcomes update playbooks while experimental results generate new ideas.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:21.280
The system targets the long feedback loops and fragile infrastructure that makes serial iteration impractical from multi-day GPU jobs on large recommendation models.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.300
Playbooks gather operational knowledge by logging failed attempts alongside successful pipelines.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:37.040
Pro Medicon frames mediation as a proactive, multi-stage, party-aware process with 11 strategies and four party behavior states.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.060
It rests on 972 real world cases that carry utterance level annotations.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.420
A new mad metric tracks behavior pattern shifts turn by turn.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:55.840
Tests of several models plus a custom pro-media agent baseline show ongoing difficulty in dynamic multi-party settings.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:59.420
The data set and code are released for others to build on.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:08.760
One article explains how KV caching keeps key and value vectors from earlier tokens, so the model skips recomputing them for each new token.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.000
The write-up covers the memory bandwidth trade-off and why the technique matters for practical inference speed.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.960
It also notes how the cache size grows with sequence length and what that means for hardware choices at deployment time.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:29.880
A thread on R-slash local LLAMA looks at which models still run usefully on eight gigabytes of VRAM.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:39.160
Participants focus on office tasks, embeddings, re-ranking and chat, stressing tool use and multilingual performance over broad world knowledge.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.380
Vision features often take a backseat when hardware forces trade-offs.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:51.600
Flex comp trains one compressor that handles any memory budget K by sampling different budgets during training in a matrioshka style.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.540
At inference time, either a confidence cascade or a lightweight learned predictor picks the right K for each input.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:11.180
The single model matches the accuracy of separately trained fixed ratio specialists while cutting KV cache use by 50% and lifting decoding throughput by 47% at serving batch sizes.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:19.160
The cascade keeps over 98% of the mildest ratios accuracy at up to 266 times average compression.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:29.980
The learned predictor reaches 158 to 236 times compression while staying within 0.7 F1 of the mildest ratio in one pass.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:40.880
Pop the hood on context compression and flex comp shows a single set of weights can handle many ratios because the memory budget K gets sampled per instance during training.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.640
Instead of being locked at architecture time.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:52.080
The matrioshka nesting means lower K prefixes of the memory tokens stay useful even when the model saw higher budgets in training.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:07:01.260
At serving time, the system routes either through the cascade until a confidence threshold is reached or through the small learned predictor in one forward pass.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:09.360
The single pass predictor stays within 0.7 F1 while hitting 158 to 236 times compression.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:17.820
Quality at high compression is not uniform across inputs so some documents keep accuracy at far higher ratios than others.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:25.880
Teams reach for the cascade when an extra model call fits the latency budget and for the learned predictor when throughput is the tightest limit.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:35.740
Very high ratios still need the base model to have seen enough variety in K during training or the low K regime drops faster than the reported numbers indicate.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:42.660
Read the complete and thropic threat report and compare the misuse patterns against the guardrails in your own application.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:51.220
Test flex comp style per input compression on your longest context workloads to measure KV cache savings against accuracy.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:58.960
Run the search Atlas graph parser on your own agent traces to find evidence flow failures that accuracy metrics overlook.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:07.620
Apply the cmni schema to any Chinese domain extraction task to see where current zero shot models fail on span boundaries.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:13.540
More labs are expected to issue similar detailed misuse reports after anthropics example.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:20.620
Additional agent benchmarks that score process traces instead of final answers are likely in the coming weeks.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:28.180
Further work on variable ratio context compression is anticipated as inference costs stays a primary constraint.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:36.440
Before we go watch for expanded low resource language evaluation sets as researchers keep testing multilingual generation limits.

